# ONE GPC, ONE DISPLAY -- the configuration with SLACK, which is its point.
#
#   cd discretePanel
#   YAGPC_PACETRACE=1 GPCS=1 CRTS=1 \
#       ~/workspace/pass-run/synctest.sh ... 10 light-1gpc
#
# THE OPPOSITE OF bench-1gpc.script, deliberately.  That one runs three
# displays to be CPU-bound, because a benchmark wants the machine saturated;
# its header says in terms not to use YAGPC_PACETRACE with it, since four
# clock reads per step compressed a real 4.7% gain to 1.5%.
#
# But that cost only matters where there is nothing to spare.  Given headroom
# the instrumentation stops changing the answer, and it is the only thing
# that says WHERE the wall time went -- idle loop, sleeps, rebases and their
# causes -- rather than merely how much of it there was.  Knowing how far
# behind a configuration runs does not say what to fix; this does.
#
# At OPS 0 with one display this is the lightest real workload there is, and
# directly comparable to the 48%-of-a-core figure measured for 1 GPC at OPS 0
# earlier in this work.
+0     gpc 1
+0.01  mode HALT
+0.02  idppower 1 on
+0.01  script ipl-one-gpc.script gpc=1 crt=1 idp=1 kb=KB1
